'=============================================================================
'
'  Stratus AS3935 Lightning Detection System
'  Logging the ASCII serial records sent by the RPi Zero W LDS
'
'  Property of METRON (PTY) LTD [Inteltronics]
'  Developer: L.J. Esterhuizen
'
'=============================================================================
' Site: QUAGGASKLIP
'
' 9600 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit, 3.3 V TTL
'
'   L,<distance_km>,<energy>     one record per forwarded strike
'   H,<cpu_temp_c>,<rssi_dbm>    status, every campbell_heartbeat_interval
'                                (600 s in quaggasklip_config.json)
'
' Both records end with a real CR LF. distance_km is -1 when the AS3935 reports
' a strike it cannot range, so -1 is never treated as a "closest strike".
'
' The status record is what makes a dead detector visible. A quiet sky and a
' crashed Pi both produce zero L records, so without H there is nothing to tell
' them apart. This program therefore captures whole lines and dispatches on the
' first character, rather than filtering the serial input on a BeginWord of "L".
'
' WIRING
'
' Everything lands on the Terminal 2 Click, which sits in mikroBUS socket 2 of
' the Pi 2 Click Shield and breaks that socket out to two screw blocks. TB1
' carries AN, RST, CS, SCK, MISO, MOSI and 3V3; TB2 carries PWM, INT, TX, RX,
' SCL, SDA and 5V.
'
'   Terminal 2 Click TB2 "TX"    Pi BCM 14   ->  CR300  C2
'   Terminal 2 Click either GND  Pi GND      ->  CR300  G
'
' Ground is not optional. Without a shared reference the receiver samples noise
' instead of data, and the symptom is ParseErrorCount climbing steadily while
' everything else looks healthy.
'
' No level shifter is needed. Campbell specifies C1 and C2 as 3.3 V input (they
' output 5.0 V, but that is irrelevant here since the logger only receives),
' with an input tolerance of -10 V to +15 V. The Pi drives 3.3 V, which is
' exactly the specified level. P_SW is likewise 3.3 V, tolerant to +/-17 V.
'
' BCM 14 is the Pi's hardware UART, which is what the Terminal 2 Click exists to
' bring out. install.sh frees it: it removes console=serial0 from cmdline.txt and
' disables the serial getty, so the pin is the detector's and not the console's.
' It also disables Bluetooth, which moves /dev/serial0 onto the PL011 so the baud
' rate stops drifting with the core clock.
'
' If for some reason the boot config cannot be changed, the detector has a
' bit-bang fallback on BCM 26. That is socket 2's INT, so it comes out on TB2
' pin 3, and it needs campbell_transport set to "bitbang" plus pigpiod running.
' Prefer the hardware UART: it costs no CPU and does not compete with the strike
' interrupt on a single-core Zero W.
'
' ENABLE THE PI SIDE FIRST, or this program will log nothing at all:
'
'   /home/quaggasklip/quaggasklip_config.json  ->  "campbell_enabled": true
'                                                  "campbell_transport": "serial"
'   sudo systemctl restart lightning-detector
'
' PULSE INPUT, second wire, currently DISABLED on this unit
'
'   Terminal 2 Click TB1 pin 7 "RST"   Pi BCM 19   ->  CR300  P_SW
'
' The detector can mirror every confirmed strike as one pulse on BCM 19, set by
' "pulse_mirror_enabled" and "pulse_mirror_ms" in quaggasklip_config.json. It
' ships disabled, so nothing arrives on P_SW until it is turned on. The default
' width is 25 ms, well above any switch-closure debounce and still good for 10
' strikes per second, far beyond anything this unit forwards.
'
' BCM 19 is socket 2's RST line. It has to be a socket 2 pin to reach a terminal
' at all: socket 1 holds the Thunder Click, so socket 1's PWM on BCM 18 dead-ends
' under that board. The other usable choice is socket 2's PWM on BCM 17, TB2
' pin 2. If you move to it, change pulse_mirror_pin and this comment together.
'
' This is a deliberately independent count. It is driven straight off the strike
' handler, so it does not depend on the serial line, the network, or the panel.
' PulseCountTotal and StrikeCount should track each other closely, and the two
' diverging is a real signal rather than a nuisance:
'
'   pulses >  serial   the serial line or its wiring is dropping records, or the
'                      interference guard is muting strikes, which it does while
'                      still emitting the pulse
'   serial >  pulses   the pulse wire, the P_SW terminal or its ground is at
'                      fault, since the serial path proved the strike was real
'
' Both counts come from the same confirmed lightning interrupts. Disturber and
' noise interrupts never produce a pulse.
'=============================================================================

'--- Scan timing -------------------------------------------------------------
' Liveness is counted in scans rather than read from the clock, so it survives
' a time set from the WiFi connection. Deriving the divisor from SCAN_MS keeps
' that arithmetic correct if the scan rate is ever changed.
Const SCAN_MS = 200
Const SCANS_PER_MIN = 60000 / SCAN_MS

' How long without any record before the detector is declared offline. The Pi
' sends status every 10 minutes, so 25 covers two missed records plus margin.
' Deliberately shorter than the admin panel's own 130 minute threshold: the
' logger is the local, independent witness.
Const OFFLINE_AFTER_MIN = 25

'--- Lightning, from L records ----------------------------------------------
Public LastDistanceKm As Float
Public LastEnergy As Long
Public StrikeCount As Long
Public StrikesToday As Long
Public ClosestTodayKm As Float
Public MaxEnergyToday As Long
Public PulseCountScan As Float
Public PulseCountTotal As Float

'--- Detector health, from H records ----------------------------------------
Public PiCpuTempC As Float
Public PiRssiDbm As Float
Public StatusCount As Long
Public MinutesSinceRecord As Float
Public DetectorOnline As Boolean

' Pulse count minus serial count. Expected to sit at or near zero. Drifting
' positive means the serial path is losing records or the interference guard is
' muting them; drifting negative means the pulse wire or P_SW is at fault. See
' the PULSE INPUT note in the header.
Public CountMismatch As Float

'--- Parsing ---------------------------------------------------------------
Public LastRecord As String * 48
Public RecordType As String * 2
Public BytesReturned As Float
Public Parsed(3) As Float
Public ParseErrorCount As Long

Dim RecordOK As Boolean
Dim ScansSinceRecord As Long

'=============================================================================
' DATA TABLES
'
' Every table is called on every scan. DataInterval decides when a row is
' actually written. This matters: output processing such as Totalize and
' Maximum accumulates once per CallTable, so gating CallTable behind IfTime
' would leave the daily aggregates built from a single sample per day.
' LightningEvents is the exception, being event driven with no DataInterval,
' so it is called only when a strike record parses.
'=============================================================================

' One row per strike. Nothing is averaged away.
DataTable (LightningEvents, True, -1)
  Sample (1, LastDistanceKm, FP2)
  Sample (1, LastEnergy, Long)
  Sample (1, PulseCountTotal, FP2)
  Sample (1, LastRecord, String)
EndTable

' The once-a-minute LightningHealth table was REMOVED.
'
' It logged the detector's own housekeeping: Pi CPU temperature, wifi RSSI, an
' online flag, minutes since the last record, and running record/error counts.
' That is 1440 rows a day describing the Pi rather than the lightning, on a
' logger whose actual subject may produce nothing for weeks.
'
' It was also a second copy of something this logger is not the system of record
' for. The Pi reports its own CPU temperature, load and RSSI straight to the
' lightning console on its hourly heartbeat, and the console is where the unit's
' health is read. Two sources for one fact is how they end up disagreeing.
'
' Everything it sampled is still computed and still Public, so DetectorOnline,
' PiCpuTempC, PiRssiDbm, CountMismatch and the counters remain visible in the
' logger's live numeric display for troubleshooting on site. They are simply not
' written to storage.
'
' What this logger records is lightning: distance, energy and the mirrored IRQ
' pulse.

' Daily summary, the same figures the monthly report quotes. Today's values are
' tracked in their own variables and reset at midnight, so a day with no
' strikes reports NAN and zero rather than carrying yesterday's numbers
' forward.
DataTable (LightningDaily, True, -1)
  DataInterval (0, 1, Day, 10)
  Sample (1, StrikesToday, Long)
  Sample (1, ClosestTodayKm, FP2)
  Sample (1, MaxEnergyToday, Long)
  Totalize (1, PulseCountScan, FP2, False)
EndTable

'=============================================================================
' PROGRAM
'=============================================================================

BeginProg
  ClosestTodayKm = NAN
  MaxEnergyToday = 0
  StrikesToday = 0
  DetectorOnline = False
  ScansSinceRecord = 0

  ' Receive only, ASCII, 8N1. A 256 byte buffer holds roughly a dozen records,
  ' which absorbs a strike burst arriving faster than the scan drains it.
  SerialOpen (ComC2_Rx, 9600, 0, 0, 256)
  SerialFlush (ComC2_Rx)

  Scan (SCAN_MS, mSec, 0, 0)

    ' RecordOK describes THIS scan, so it is cleared at the top of every scan.
    ' It used to be cleared only inside the "a record arrived" branch below,
    ' which left it holding last scan's answer on any scan where the UART was
    ' quiet. Nothing depended on that until the pulse-only output below started
    ' testing it, at which point a stale True would have suppressed exactly the
    ' rows that output exists to capture.
    RecordOK = False

    '--- Pulse input, one 50 ms pulse per confirmed strike -----------------
    ' PConfig 2 = switch closure up to 150 Hz, POption 0 = counts. This is the
    ' independent strike count, see the PULSE INPUT note in the header.
    PulseCount (PulseCountScan, 1, P_SW, 2, 0, 1, 0)
    PulseCountTotal = PulseCountTotal + PulseCountScan

    '--- Serial records ---------------------------------------------------
    ' BeginWord 0 captures the whole line including its leading letter, so one
    ' call handles both record types. EndWord 3338 is &H0D0A, that is CR LF.
    ' Option 10 takes the OLDEST buffered record and leaves the destination
    ' untouched when nothing arrived, which drains a burst in order. Option 01
    ' would take only the newest record and discard the rest of the burst.
    SerialInRecord (ComC2_Rx, LastRecord, 0, 0, 3338, BytesReturned, 10)

    If BytesReturned > 0 Then
      RecordType = Left (LastRecord, 1)
      ' Field 1 is the leading letter and parses as NAN. Fields 2 and 3 carry
      ' the values.
      SplitStr (Parsed, LastRecord, ",", 3, 0)

      ' x = x is false only for NAN, which is how a failed parse is caught.
      If Parsed(2) = Parsed(2) AND Parsed(3) = Parsed(3) Then

        If RecordType = "L" Then
          LastDistanceKm = Parsed(2)
          LastEnergy = Parsed(3)
          StrikeCount = StrikeCount + 1
          StrikesToday = StrikesToday + 1

          If LastEnergy > MaxEnergyToday Then MaxEnergyToday = LastEnergy

          ' -1 means the strike could not be ranged, so it is not a candidate
          ' for the closest approach of the day.
          If LastDistanceKm >= 0 Then
            If ClosestTodayKm <> ClosestTodayKm Then
              ClosestTodayKm = LastDistanceKm
            ElseIf LastDistanceKm < ClosestTodayKm Then
              ClosestTodayKm = LastDistanceKm
            EndIf
          EndIf

          RecordOK = True
          CallTable LightningEvents
        EndIf

        If RecordType = "H" Then
          PiCpuTempC = Parsed(2)
          PiRssiDbm = Parsed(3)
          StatusCount = StatusCount + 1
          RecordOK = True
        EndIf

      EndIf

      If RecordOK Then
        ' Any valid record proves the detector is alive, strike or status.
        ScansSinceRecord = 0
        DetectorOnline = True
      Else
        ParseErrorCount = ParseErrorCount + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf

    '--- Liveness ---------------------------------------------------------
    ScansSinceRecord = ScansSinceRecord + 1
    MinutesSinceRecord = ScansSinceRecord / SCANS_PER_MIN
    If MinutesSinceRecord > OFFLINE_AFTER_MIN Then
      DetectorOnline = False
    EndIf

    '--- Cross-check the two independent strike paths ----------------------
    CountMismatch = PulseCountTotal - StrikeCount

    '--- Output -----------------------------------------------------------
    ' A mirrored IRQ pulse with no serial record still deserves a row. The pulse
    ' is the hardware witness that the sensor fired; the serial record is the
    ' software account of it. If the UART drops a message the pulse is the only
    ' evidence left, and losing it would make a real strike invisible.
    If PulseCountScan > 0 And Not RecordOK Then
      CallTable LightningEvents
    EndIf

    ' LightningDaily is still called every scan on purpose: its Totalize
    ' accumulates once per CallTable, so gating this behind IfTime would build the
    ' daily total from a single sample per day. DataInterval decides when the row
    ' is written.
    CallTable LightningDaily

    ' Reset today's figures after the daily row has been written above.
    If IfTime (0, 1, Day) Then
      StrikesToday = 0
      ClosestTodayKm = NAN
      MaxEnergyToday = 0
    EndIf

  NextScan
EndProg
